    "year_opened": 1920,
    "era": "historic",
    "color": "#b45309",
    "corridor_confidence": "medium",
    "description": "WWI British metre-gauge line; later superseded by standard gauge",
    "notes": "The British military supply railway of the WWI Mesopotamian campaign.  [historic_vs_modern] This is the WWI-era METRE-GAUGE (1,000 mm) Basra-Baghdad line (via Nasiriyah), completed 16 Jan 1920. It is DISTINCT from the modern STANDARD-gauge Baghdad-Basra line (freight 1964, passengers 1968) via Kut, which is represented as the southern end of the Baghdad Railway route.  [route] Some WWI branches (Qurna-Amara, Sheikh Saad-Sinn, Baghdad-Table Mountain) were 2 ft 6 in (762 mm), not metre gauge.",
    "history": "Built by the British military during the WWI Mesopotamian campaign; the Basra-Nasiriyah section was the most important wartime line, extended north so the full Basra-Baghdad route (metre gauge) opened on 16 January 1920.",
